<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bridei son of Maelchon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 584</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Bridei I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bridei son of Maelchon was a powerful king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> in the second half of the 6th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-columba">Life of Columba</a> describes the saint visiting him at his fortress near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-ness">River Ness</a> in the 560s. Irish annals record his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> of Dal Riata around 558 and his death around 584.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-maelchon.jpg" alt="Bridei son of Maelchon" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Hole · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Columba_converting_the_Picts.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bridei's name appears in several forms, among them Brude son of Melcho and, in Irish sources, Bruide son of Maelchú. Little is known of his origins and the sources about him are vague or contradictory. The writer John Morris, in The Age of Arthur, made him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a>, king of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, but historians regard that book as unreliable. An entry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> reporting his death under the year 505 is presumed to be an error.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He first appears in the Irish annals between 558 and 560, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> report a migration before King Bruide son of Máelchú. The Ulster annalist does not say who fled, but the later Annals of Tigernach speak of the flight of the Scots before him in 558, and one version of the Annals of Ulster links this with the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabran-mac-domangairt">Gabrán mac Domangairt</a>. The Annals of Ulster also report expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> in 580 and 581, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> mentions an under-king of Orkney at Bridei's court.</p><p>Bridei was not the only king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> of his day: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record the death of Galam Cennalath, king of the Picts, in 580. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>'s Life of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> treats him as one of the chief kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> but does not say whether he was already a Christian or whether Columba converted him. Finds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portmahomack">Portmahomack</a>, showing a monastic community there from around 550, lend some support to the view that he was Christian at least in name.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craig-phadrig">Craig Phadrig</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a> above the Beauly Firth, has been suggested as the steep rock on which, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>'s account, his court stood, but its ramparts were in poor repair in Bridei's time, so it is unlikely to have been a royal fortress then. His kingdom may have corresponded to the later Fortriu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bridei died in the 580s, perhaps in battle against Pictish rivals in Circinn, an area thought to correspond to the Mearns. The king lists of the Pictish Chronicle agree that he was followed by Gartnait son of Domelch. Juliet Marillier's trilogy The Bridei Chronicles mixes history, fiction and informed guesswork in telling his rise and rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bridei I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kincardineshire">Kincardineshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.92,-2.5&amp;year=574" aria-label="Kincardineshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Bridei son of Maelchon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4883 5261h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4883 5261h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=56.92,-2.5&amp;year=574">What was here in 574 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
